RECEIPTS - component-by-component at the KIMI standard:
1. RKHS INJECTIVITY: VERIFIED. For compactly supported finite-order
   distributions T, h_T = T_yK(·,y) = 0 implies T = 0. Planar case:
   K̂ > 0 everywhere, standard. Periodized case (the one the theorem
   needs): atomic lattice spectrum with weights e^{−2π²|k|²/L²} > 0 at
   every lattice point; h_T = 0 forces all Fourier coefficients of T to
   vanish, and Fourier uniqueness for distributions on a compact torus
   gives T = 0. The manuscript's integral display is the planar form;
   the lattice-sum analogue it invokes is correct. Independently
   consistent with my G.1/DER-002 route (trigonometric polynomial on
   the full dual lattice ⇒ coefficients zero).
2. INTERIOR ADJOINT FORMULA: VERIFIED EXACTLY. First variation
   η̇ = H_f η + ∇h(γ); adjoint −ẇ = H_fᵀw; then
   d/dt(w·η) = ẇ·η + w·η̇ = w·∇h(γ) (H symmetric) - the same identity
   class as EC-011 (adjoint-orbit conservation), which I verified
   during the corpus integration. The curve contribution and its
   nonzero density (ODE uniqueness: a nonzero adjoint solution cannot
   vanish at an isolated time) are correct.
3. ENDPOINT VARIATION: δp = −H_f(p)^{-1}∇h(p) VERIFIED EXACTLY
   (implicit function theorem on ∇(f+εh)(p+εδp) = 0). The launch-point
   variation is a finite combination of the endpoint two-jets - finite
   order, supported on {p,q} - structurally correct.
4. SUPPORT-SEPARATION NONVANISHING (Proposition 6.1): VERIFIED, with an
   explicit construction the manuscript only sketches. If T_f = 0,
   then for any test ψ supported near one interior orbit point and
   disjoint from {p,q}, the endpoint atoms vanish on ψ, so
   T_f(ψ) = ∫ a(t)n(t)·∇ψ(γ(t))dt. Choose ψ = χ(s_n) with s_n the
   signed transverse coordinate in a tube and χ' > 0 fixed: the
   integrand keeps a definite sign on a short interval around the
   chosen point (a(t)n(t) continuous, nonzero there), so the integral
   is nonzero - contradiction. The endpoint-overlap worry (the orbit
   approaches p, q, so supports touch in the limit) is correctly
   handled by the disjoint-support test: the curve density is nonzero
   on the OPEN segment, where no endpoint support exists. The density
   is nonzero everywhere by ODE uniqueness, hence G_f ≠ 0. VERIFIED.
5. ENDPOINT CONVERGENCE: the adjoint weight decays exponentially toward
   the saddles; the RKHS norm of ∇₂K(·,γ(t)) is bounded; the curve
   integral converges absolutely in H. The arclength weight s^{ν−1},
   ν = |λ_transverse|/λ_departure > 0, is integrable on [0,1] -
   VERIFIED. The s^{−5/6}-type quadrature failures are correctly
   classified as numerical-method artifacts, not existence issues.
6. ONE-DIMENSIONAL SLICING: VERIFIED. CM decomposition f = g + ξh_j
   with ξ an independent nondegenerate 1D Gaussian (standard for
   Gaussian fields on Banach spaces, including the periodized field);
   F_g(ξ) = D_χ(g+ξh_j); F_g(ξ₀) = 0 with F'_g(ξ₀) = dD_χ[h_j] ≠ 0
   (nonzero by construction of chart+direction) ⇒ regular ⇒ isolated
   zeros ⇒ discrete ⇒ countable ⇒ Lebesgue-null; conditional density
   ⇒ conditional probability zero; integrate over g ⇒ probability zero.
